Sound

Sound reimplements, in Crow, the behaviour of Tone.js, Copyright (c) 2014
Yotam Mann, licensed under the MIT License (https://github.com/Tonejs/Tone.js):
its time and pitch notation, signals and their ramps, envelopes,
oscillators, noise, players, synths, filters, effects, dynamics, channels,
analysers and transport, held to the numbers and the renders Tone.js makes.
No Tone.js source is included; its behaviour was read from version 15.1.22,
and from running it in Chrome, and written anew.

What Tone.js leaves to the page is done here as the Web Audio API
specification (W3C, https://www.w3.org/TR/webaudio/) says the page does it:
the biquad filters' coefficients, the oscillators' wavetables, the panners'
equal-power law, distance models and cones, the convolver's scaling, the
analyser's window and smoothing, the compressor's curve, and how a loop of
nodes comes round a render quantum late. Positional sound follows three.js's
PositionalAudio (MIT License, Copyright the three.js authors), whose
defaults are the specification's PannerNode's.

Numbers that are a method's own, written anew from their published
descriptions:

  - Freeverb's comb and allpass tunings, by Jezar at Dreampoint (public
    domain).
  - The comb delays and allpass frequencies of the Schroeder reverberator
    John Chowning's CCRMA work laid out, as Tone's JCReverb and STK's
    JCRev use them.
  - Paul Kellet's filter for pink noise (published to the music-dsp list,
    free to use).
